al_loss=0.5675  val_acc=0.9525  lr=0.00e+00\n======================================================================\nBest validation accuracy: 0.9533 at epoch 19\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":22},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Training Curves\n\nPlotting the loss and accuracy over epochs is the best way to diagnose training problems:\n\n- **Both losses decreasing** - training is working normally.\n- **Train loss low but val loss rising** - overfitting. The model is memorising training images. Try more augmentation or reducing learning rate.\n- **Both losses plateau early** - underfitting. Try a higher learning rate or more epochs.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot training curves\nepochs_x = range(1, len(history['train_loss']) + 1)\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\n\n# Loss curve\naxes[0].plot(epochs_x, history['train_loss'], marker='o', ms=4, label='Train Loss')\naxes[0].plot(epochs_x, history['val_loss'],   marker='s', ms=4, label='Val Loss')\naxes[0].axvline(best_epoch + 1, color='red', linestyle='--', alpha=0.7,\n                label=f'Best epoch ({best_epoch+1})')\naxes[0].set_title('Loss vs. Epoch', fontsize=13)\naxes[0].set_xlabel('Epoch'); axes[0].set_ylabel('Loss')\naxes[0].legend(); axes[0].grid(alpha=0.3)\naxes[0].spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n\n# Accuracy curve\naxes[1].plot(epochs_x, [a * 100 for a in history['val_acc']],\n             color='forestgreen', marker='D', ms=4, label='Val Accuracy')\naxes[1].axvline(best_epoch + 1, color='red', linestyle='--', alpha=0.7,\n                label=f'Best epoch ({best_epoch+1})')\naxes[1].set_title('Validation Accuracy vs. Epoch', fontsize=13)\naxes[1].set_xlabel('Epoch'); axes[1].set_ylabel('Accuracy (%)')\naxes[1].legend(); axes[1].grid(alpha=0.3)\naxes[1].spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('training_curves.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-15T16:06:26.998519Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-15T16:06:26.998805Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-15T16:06:27.709291Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-15T16:06:26.998766Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-15T16:06:27.708462Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":23},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 6 - Evaluation\n\nTraining is done. Now we load the **best checkpoint** (the epoch with the highest validation accuracy that was saved earlier) and do a thorough evaluation.\n\n**Metrics we compute:**\n\n- **Top-1 Accuracy** - the fraction of images where the model's top prediction is correct. This is the competition's primary metric.\n- **Macro F1** - computes the F1 score for each pen class separately and averages them. This penalises models that do well on common pens but fail on rare ones.\n\n**Confusion matrix** - a grid where row = true pen, column = predicted pen. A perfect model fills only the main diagonal. Off-diagonal numbers reveal which pen pairs the model most often confuses.\n\n**Misclassified examples** - we show actual images the model got wrong, together with what it predicted and how confident it was. This often reveals whether the model is making sensible mistakes (confusing two visually similar pens) or nonsensical ones.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load the best checkpoint\ncheckpoint = torch.load(CHECKPOINT, map_location=DEVICE)\nraw_state = checkpoint['model_state']\n\nfirst_key = next(iter(raw_state))\nif not first_key.startswith('backbone.'):\n    remapped = {}\n    for k, v in raw_state.items():\n        if k.startswith('head.'):          # custom LayerNorm/Linear head\n            remapped[k] = v\n        else:                              # backbone weights → add prefix\n            remapped['backbone.' + k] = v\n    raw_state = remapped\n    print('State dict remapped: added backbone. prefix to backbone keys.')\n\nmodel.load_state_dict(raw_state)\nmodel.eval()\nprint(f\"Loaded checkpoint from epoch {checkpoint['epoch']+1}  \"\n      f\"(saved val_acc = {checkpoint['val_acc']:.4f})\")\n\n# Collect all predictions on the validation set\nall_preds, all_labels, all_probs = [], [], []\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for imgs, labels in tqdm(val_loader, desc='Evaluating'):\n        imgs = imgs.to(DEVICE, non_blocking=True)\n        with autocast(enabled=(DEVICE.type == 'cuda')):\n            logits = model(imgs)\n        probs = torch.softmax(logits, dim=1).cpu().numpy()\n        preds = probs.argmax(axis=1)\n        all_probs.extend(probs)\n        all_preds.extend(preds)\n        all_labels.extend(labels.numpy())\n\nall_preds  = np.array(all_preds)\nall_labels = np.array(all_labels)\nall_probs  = np.array(all_probs)\n\naccuracy = (all_preds == all_labels).mean()\nmacro_f1 = f1_score(all_labels, all_preds, average='macro')\n\nprint(f'\\n{\"─\"*45}')\nprint(f'  Validation Accuracy : {accuracy:.4f}  ({accuracy*100:.2f}%)')\nprint(f'  Macro F1 Score      : {macro_f1:.4f}')\nprint(f'{\"─\"*45}')\n\nclass_names = [f'pen_{i+1}' for i in range(NUM_CLASSES)]\nprint('\\nPer-class accuracy:')\nfor c in range(NUM_CLASSES):\n    mask    = all_labels == c\n    cls_acc = (all_preds[mask] == c).mean() if mask.sum() > 0 else 0.0\n    bar_fill = int(cls_acc * 30)\n    print(f'  {class_names[c]}: {cls_acc:.4f}  [{\"#\"*bar_fill}{\".\"*(30-bar_fill)}]')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-15T16:06:27.710458Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-15T16:06:27.711108Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-15T16:06:42.263304Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-15T16:06:27.711081Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-15T16:06:42.262427Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Loaded checkpoint from epoch 19  (saved val_acc = 0.9533)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"Evaluating:   0%|          | 0/252 [00:00<?, ?it/s]","application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"version_major":2,"version_minor":0,"model_id":"c44ed1919acd4159945e488d499e8eec"}},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"\n─────────────────────────────────────────────\n  Validation Accuracy : 0.9533  (95.33%)\n  Macro F1 Score      : 0.9531\n─────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nPer-class accuracy:\n  pen_1: 0.9931  [#############################.]\n  pen_2: 1.0000  [##############################]\n  pen_3: 0.8434  [#########################.....]\n  pen_4: 1.0000  [##############################]\n  pen_5: 0.9971  [#############################.]\n  pen_6: 1.0000  [##############################]\n  pen_7: 0.8257  [########################......]\n  pen_8: 0.9653  [############################..]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":24},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The confusion matrix below shows where the model makes mistakes. Each cell (row r, column c) contains the number of images from true pen r that the model predicted as pen c. A large number outside the diagonal means those two pens are frequently confused with each other.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Confusion matrix\ncm = confusion_matrix(all_labels, all_preds)\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 7))\ndisp = ConfusionMatrixDisplay(confusion_matrix=cm, display_labels=class_names)\ndisp.plot(ax=ax, colorbar=True, cmap='Blues', values_format='d')\nax.set_title(\n    f'Confusion Matrix  |  Acc={accuracy:.4f}  Macro-F1={macro_f1:.4f}',\n    fontsize=12, pad=12\n)\nax.set_xlabel('Predicted Pen', fontsize=11)\nax.set_ylabel('True Pen', fontsize=11)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('confusion_matrix.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()\n\n# Most confused pairs\nprint('Top 5 most confused pen pairs (excluding diagonal):')\ncm_off = cm.copy()\nnp.fill_diagonal(cm_off, 0)\nfor rank in range(5):\n    r, c = np.unravel_index(cm_off.argmax(), cm_off.shape)\n    print(f'  {rank+1}. True=pen_{r+1}  Predicted=pen_{c+1}  Count={cm_off[r,c]}')\n    cm_off[r, c] = 0","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-15T16:06:42.265001Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-15T16:06:42.265277Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-15T16:06:52.913260Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-15T16:06:42.265240Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-15T16:06:52.912511Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x700 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Top 5 most confused pen pairs (excluding diagonal):\n  1. True=pen_7  Predicted=pen_3  Count=149\n  2. True=pen_3  Predicted=pen_7  Count=135\n  3. True=pen_7  Predicted=pen_8  Count=22\n  4. True=pen_3  Predicted=pen_8  Count=20\n  5. True=pen_8  Predicted=pen_3  Count=20\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":25},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Looking at actual misclassified images helps us understand whether the model's errors are reasonable. Each image below shows the true pen label, the model's wrong prediction, and the confidence score (how sure the model was — higher is worse for wrong predictions).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Visualise misclassified examples\nmisclassified_idx = np.where(all_preds != all_labels)[0]\nprint(f'Misclassified: {len(misclassified_idx)} / {len(all_labels)} '\n      f'({len(misclassified_idx)/len(all_labels)*100:.1f}%)')\n\nN_SHOW = min(16, len(misclassified_idx))\nchosen = np.random.choice(misclassified_idx, N_SHOW, replace=False)\nCOLS   = 4\nROWS   = (N_SHOW + COLS - 1) // COLS\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(ROWS, COLS, figsize=(COLS * 3.2, ROWS * 3.2))\naxes      = axes.flatten()\n\nfor plot_i, val_i in enumerate(chosen):\n    row  = val_split.iloc[val_i]\n    path = DATA_DIR / row['image_path']\n    ax   = axes[plot_i]\n\n    if path.exists():\n        ax.imshow(Image.open(path).convert('RGB'))\n    else:\n        ax.set_facecolor('#ddd')\n\n    true_pen = class_names[all_labels[val_i]]\n    pred_pen = class_names[all_preds[val_i]]\n    conf     = all_probs[val_i, all_preds[val_i]] * 100\n    ax.set_title(f'True: {true_pen}\\nPred: {pred_pen} ({conf:.0f}% conf)',\n                 fontsize=8.5, color='crimson')\n    ax.axis('off')\n\nfor ax in axes[N_SHOW:]:\n    ax.axis('off')\n\nfig.suptitle('Misclassified Images — True Label vs. Model Prediction',\n             fontsize=13, y=1.01)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('misclassified.png', dpi=80, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-15T16:06:52.914356Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-15T16:06:52.914582Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.024889Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-15T16:06:52.914562Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.024124Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Misclassified: 376 / 8050 (4.7%)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1280x1280 with 16 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":26},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 7 - Test-Time Augmentation (TTA) and Submission\n\n### What is Test-Time Augmentation?\n\nNormally during inference we feed one clean version of each test image into the model and take whatever prediction comes out. TTA does something smarter: for each test image we create **5 different versions** of it (one centre-crop and four randomly augmented crops and flips), run all 5 through the model, and **average the probability scores** before making the final decision.\n\nThink of it like asking 5 different experts to look at the same circle drawing (each from a slightly different angle or zoom level) and then taking the majority vote. The average is more reliable than any single view alone, especially for borderline cases where the ink texture might be slightly obscured in one crop but visible in another.\n\n**Why it works:** A circle drawn with pen 3 is still drawn with pen 3 when you flip it or look at a different part of it. The pen's ink texture appears everywhere in the circle. Averaging over 5 views reduces the noise in any single prediction.\n\nTypical accuracy gain from TTA: **+0.3% to +1.0%**, with no extra training needed.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"class TTADataset(Dataset):\n    '''\n    Wraps the test DataFrame and returns TTA_STEPS augmented views for each image.\n\n    __getitem__ returns (list_of_tensors, image_id).\n    The first tensor is always a clean centre-crop (no randomness).\n    The remaining tensors are randomly augmented crops and flips.\n    '''\n\n    def __init__(self, df: pd.DataFrame, data_dir: Path,\n                 base_transform, tta_transform, tta_steps: int = TTA_STEPS):\n        self.df            = df.reset_index(drop=True)\n        self.data_dir      = data_dir\n        self.base_transform = base_transform  # Clean, deterministic crop\n        self.tta_transform  = tta_transform   # Random augmented crops\n        self.tta_steps     = tta_steps\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.df)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, idx: int):\n        row  = self.df.iloc[idx]\n        path = self.data_dir / row['image_path']\n        img  = np.array(Image.open(path).convert('RGB')) if path.exists() \\\n               else np.full((IMG_SIZE, IMG_SIZE, 3), 128, dtype=np.uint8)\n\n        # View 1: clean centre-crop (deterministic)\n        views = [self.base_transform(image=img)['image']]\n        # Views 2-5: random augmented crops\n        for _ in range(self.tta_steps - 1):\n            views.append(self.tta_transform(image=img)['image'])\n\n        return views, str(row['image_id'])\n\n\n# TTA augmentation pipeline (lighter than training)\ntta_aug = A.Compose([\n    A.RandomResizedCrop(size=(IMG_SIZE, IMG_SIZE),\n                        scale=(0.85, 1.0), p=1.0),\n    A.HorizontalFlip(p=0.5),\n    A.VerticalFlip(p=0.3),\n    A.Rotate(limit=20, p=0.4),\n    A.Normalize(mean=IMAGENET_MEAN, std=IMAGENET_STD),\n    ToTensorV2(),\n])\n\ntta_dataset = TTADataset(\n    test_df, DATA_DIR,\n    base_transform=val_transform,\n    tta_transform=tta_aug,\n    tta_steps=TTA_STEPS,\n)\n\n# batch_size=1 because each item is a list of tensors (not a single tensor)\ntta_loader = DataLoader(\n    tta_dataset, batch_size=1, shuffle=False,\n    num_workers=NUM_WORKERS, collate_fn=lambda x: x\n)\n\nprint(f'TTA dataset: {len(tta_dataset)} test images x {TTA_STEPS} views each')\nprint(f'Total model forward passes: {len(tta_dataset) * TTA_STEPS:,}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.026235Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.026546Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.040766Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.026513Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.040199Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"TTA dataset: 5905 test images x 5 views each\nTotal model forward passes: 29,525\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":27},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Now we run inference. For each test image:\n1. Generate 5 views (1 clean + 4 augmented)\n2. Run each view through the model to get 8 probability scores\n3. Add the 5 probability arrays together and divide by 5 (average)\n4. Take the argmax of the averaged probabilities as the final prediction\n5. Convert from 0-indexed (0-7) back to pen_id (1-8)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# TTA inference loop\nmodel.eval()\nimage_ids, final_preds, final_probs_all = [], [], []\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for batch in tqdm(tta_loader, desc='TTA Inference'):\n        # batch is a list of length 1 (because batch_size=1)\n        views_list, img_id = batch[0]\n\n        # Accumulate softmax probabilities across all TTA views\n        avg_probs = np.zeros(NUM_CLASSES, dtype=np.float32)\n\n        for view in views_list:\n            view_t = view.unsqueeze(0).to(DEVICE)  # Add batch dimension: (1, C, H, W)\n            with autocast(enabled=(DEVICE.type == 'cuda')):\n                logit = model(view_t)\n            prob = torch.softmax(logit, dim=1).cpu().numpy()[0]\n            avg_probs += prob\n\n        avg_probs /= len(views_list)   # Average over the 5 views\n\n        # argmax gives 0-7; add 1 to get pen_id 1-8\n        pred_pen_id = int(avg_probs.argmax()) + 1\n\n        image_ids.append(img_id)\n        final_preds.append(pred_pen_id)\n        final_probs_all.append(avg_probs)\n\nprint(f'Inference complete: {len(image_ids)} predictions')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.041656Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.041935Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-15T16:19:17.889716Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-15T16:07:25.041905Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-15T16:19:17.888901Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"TTA Inference:   0%|          | 0/5905 [00:00<?, ?it/s]","application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"version_major":2,"version_minor":0,"model_id":"17e1f4d1eca644c4a23e497089f3ef21"}},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Inference complete: 5905 predictions\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":28},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Building the Submission File\n\nThe competition expects a CSV with exactly two columns: `image_id` and `pen_id`. We re-order the rows to match the original order in `test.csv` (Kaggle may reject submissions where the row order differs from the sample submission). We also print a distribution chart of our predictions — if one pen class has an extreme number of predictions compared to the training distribution, something may have gone wrong.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Build submission DataFrame\nsubmission = pd.DataFrame({'image_id': image_ids, 'pen_id': final_preds})\n\n# Re-order rows to match the original test.csv order\nsubmission = (\n    submission\n    .set_index('image_id')\n    .reindex(test_df['image_id'].astype(str))\n    .reset_index()\n)\n\n# Safety net: fill any missing predictions with the most common predicted pen\nmode_pred = submission['pen_id'].mode()[0]\nsubmission['pen_id'] = submission['pen_id'].fillna(mode_pred).astype(int)\n\nsubmission.to_csv(SUBMIT_PATH, index=False)\nprint(f'Submission saved to {SUBMIT_PATH}')\nprint(f'Rows: {len(submission)}')\ndisplay(submission.head(5))\n\n# Prediction distribution chart\npred_counts = submission['pen_id'].value_counts().sort_index()\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\n\n# Test predictions\naxes[0].bar(pred_counts.index.astype(str), pred_counts.values,\n            color=palette, edgecolor='black')\naxes[0].set_title('Prediction Distribution on Test Set', fontsize=12)\naxes[0].set_xlabel('pen_id'); axes[0].set_ylabel('Count')\nfor bar, val in zip(axes[0].patches, pred_counts.values):\n    axes[0].text(bar.get_x() + bar.get_width()/2,\n                 bar.get_height() + 5, str(val),\n                 ha='center', va='bottom', fontsize=9)\n\n# Compare with training distribution\ntrain_counts = combined_df['pen_id'].value_counts().sort_index()\ntrain_pct    = train_counts / train_counts.sum()\ntest_pct     = pred_counts  / pred_counts.sum()\nx = np.arange(NUM_CLASSES)\naxes[1].bar(x - 0.2, train_pct.values * 100, 0.4,\n            label='Train distribution', color='steelblue', alpha=0.8)\naxes[1].bar(x + 0.2, test_pct.values  * 100, 0.4,\n            label='Test predictions',  color='darkorange', alpha=0.8)\naxes[1].set_xticks(x); axes[1].set_xticklabels([f'pen_{i+1}' for i in range(8)])\naxes[1].set_title('Train vs. Test Class Proportions (%)', fontsize=12)\naxes[1].set_ylabel('Percentage'); axes[1].legend()\naxes[1].spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('prediction_distribution.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()\nprint('If the orange and blue bars are similar heights, the prediction distribution looks healthy.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-15T16:19:17.891759Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-15T16:19:17.892071Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-15T16:19:28.537441Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-15T16:19:17.892034Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-15T16:19:28.536689Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Submission saved to submission_pen.csv\nRows: 5905\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"                              image_id  pen_id\n0  v2_8eb750cb7bac5c42036af72b8253976b       2\n1  v2_04e19b0acea03fe2ae474ce8a4c6b705       8\n2  v2_6b3400d5252124adcc9859cbc78c5d8a       1\n3  v2_79025cb2ef36af3dc15c91056fa225dc       8\n4  v2_3ed2f62c32c69ec191b7e5b86433cb87       5","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>image_id</th>\n      <th>pen_id</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>v2_8eb750cb7bac5c42036af72b8253976b</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>v2_04e19b0acea03fe2ae474ce8a4c6b705</td>\n      <td>8</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>v2_6b3400d5252124adcc9859cbc78c5d8a</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>v2_79025cb2ef36af3dc15c91056fa225dc</td>\n      <td>8</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>v2_3ed2f62c32c69ec191b7e5b86433cb87</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x400 with 2 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"If the orange and blue bars are similar heights, the prediction distribution looks healthy.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":29},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 8 - Ensemble: Combining Multiple Models\n\nAn ensemble combines predictions from several independently trained models. It is one of the most reliable ways to push accuracy higher on Kaggle.\n\n**Why ensembling works:** Each model makes different random mistakes depending on its architecture, random seed, and training run. When we average their probability outputs, the correct answer tends to consistently score high across models while wrong answers cancel out.\n\n**Typical accuracy gain:** +0.5% to +1.5% over a single model, with no extra labelled data required.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Train second model (convnext_tiny) for ensemble\nCHECKPOINT_B = Path('best_model_b.pth')\n\nmodel_b = CircleModel(model_name='convnext_tiny', pretrained=True).to(DEVICE)\n\nif CHECKPOINT_B.exists():\n    ckpt_b = torch.load(CHECKPOINT_B, map_location=DEVICE)\n    model_b.load_state_dict(ckpt_b['model_state'])\n    print(f\"Loaded model_b checkpoint  val_acc={ckpt_b['val_acc']:.4f}\")\nelse:\n    criterion_b = nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=LABEL_SMOOTH)\n    NO_DECAY = {'bias', 'LayerNorm.weight', 'layer_norm.weight', 'norm'}\n    param_groups_b = [\n        {'params': [p for n, p in model_b.named_parameters() if not any(nd in n for nd in NO_DECAY)], 'weight_decay': WEIGHT_DECAY},\n        {'params': [p for n, p in model_b.named_parameters() if     any(nd in n for nd in NO_DECAY)], 'weight_decay': 0.0},\n    ]\n    optimizer_b = optim.AdamW(param_groups_b, lr=LR)\n    total_steps_b  = NUM_EPOCHS * len(train_loader)\n    warmup_steps_b = int(0.05 * total_steps_b)\n    scheduler_b = optim.lr_scheduler.LambdaLR(optimizer_b,\n        lambda step: float(step) / max(1, warmup_steps_b) if step < warmup_steps_b\n                     else max(0.0, 0.5 * (1.0 + np.cos(np.pi * (step - warmup_steps_b) / max(1, total_steps_b - warmup_steps_b)))))\n    scaler_b = Gra